#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120929} :activity/id #uuid "9c55bfa3-aadc-5e0f-bea4-8915a95c985b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048163 :topic/id #uuid "84a1f6d3-985c-55c9-a161-9d6f23cb6401" :topic/math-academy-id 1815} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121014 :tutorial/content "To determine the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we first cancel any common factors in the numerator and denominator. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x$.\n\nFor example, let's try to find the vertical asymptotes of the rational function $y=\\dfrac{2}{x-5}.$ \n\nNow, there are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, and so we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =x-5 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a vertical asymptote at $x=5.$\n\nThis matches up  with what we see in the graph of the function:\n\n![](images/e9/e9d2c264e323eb86b4e56fef563387989f66fb016a3e1c98db1f35776e3b5cb5.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "22321890-d586-5e6c-bff9-b2a6b3388ae0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 264 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b0251d11-c37d-5bc9-839f-3b319b92b170" :step/math-academy-id 486583 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120930}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120930 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119954 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119695 :question/id #uuid "d462c4fb-c784-5dc3-adc1-1cd562c6a55e" :question/math-academy-id "e-84"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049074}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329835}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"} :step/id #uuid "f33e53a7-67fa-52bd-883d-157e2b1bfbc6" :step/math-academy-id 486584 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120931}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120931 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119955 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119696 :question/id #uuid "ace1e5ac-fecc-559d-923d-9fec38f410ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-85"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80b8388b-ae0b-5ea6-9efc-fed1a074f463" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050323}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329772}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions by Factoring the Numerator and Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "34f75092-da67-553a-bcea-fdad4bc5ae09" :step/math-academy-id 486587 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120932}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120932 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119956 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119697 :question/id #uuid "43990279-e10b-53dc-8f18-533f3962a935" :question/math-academy-id "e-86"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "231604dc-fe8f-59be-90fe-37af7a1bb648" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341897}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has no Vertical Asymptotes"} :step/id #uuid "3987c34c-0de9-5447-a8ac-de5808efdd56" :step/math-academy-id 486590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120933}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119957 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119699 :question/id #uuid "3b1c0748-807c-5a77-b9e8-62ce5eb03c78" :question/math-academy-id "e-87"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68ba4c5d-8b91-57bc-b98b-0e9f8d8f1f67" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050310}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341899}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Vertical Asymptotes for the Sum of Two Rational Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c1ff9805-1591-512e-a111-06479708d986" :step/math-academy-id 486593}] :activity/title "Vertical Asymptotes of Rational Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120023}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064988} :activity/id #uuid "b3e69442-b9dc-56f2-b6f4-b2d9169e67ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048434 :topic/id #uuid "9ef976b2-cf49-59ec-9689-0ec6f4a92d3c" :topic/math-academy-id 288} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065346 :tutorial/content "The **mean value theorem** states that for any function that is continuous and differentiable on some interval, there is some point where the derivative of the function is equal to the slope between the endpoints. More precisely, that is:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, then there exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.\n> $$\n\nLet's illustrate this idea with an example. Consider the graph of the function \n\n$$\ny = f(x) = 6 - \\dfrac 4 x\n$$\n\nthat's defined on the closed interval $[1,4].$\n\n![](images/fd/fd4d75f2ed0db427c2e3bd41335ceb302c3126781f595c8c21ec914e50f82f64.png)\n\nThe line $l_1$ is the secant line that intersects the curve at the endpoints of the interval. The mean value theorem guarantees that there is some special point inside the interval where the tangent line has the same slope as $l_1.$ \n\nIn the diagram above, the special point is $(2,4),$ and the tangent line $l_2$ at this point is parallel to the secant line $l_1.$\n\nTo double-check that $l_1$ and $l_2$ are parallel, we can compute their slopes. The line $l_1$ passes through the points $(1,f(1))$ and $(4,f(4)),$ so its slope is given by \n\n$$\nm=\\dfrac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} =  \\dfrac{5-2}{4-1} = 1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the slope of the line $l_2$ can be found by calculating $f'(2).$\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{4}{x^2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(2) = 1\n$$\n\nSince the lines $l_1$ and $l_2$ have the same slope, they are parallel." :tutorial/id #uuid "baf78b35-04c6-5d1c-913f-e0a3da70cd8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2659 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "10d30ee9-1327-5e6c-8d54-1b994e2a9962" :step/math-academy-id 12770 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064159 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064041 :question/id #uuid "4bbaf6b0-1057-5485-a3d8-2126e74fecdc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3717"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370618}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "50436e6a-bace-51f7-97a8-73a9025692ab" :step/math-academy-id 12771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064991}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064042 :question/id #uuid "ed796d54-235b-5ec0-b73f-5396d1ef99b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3718"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050572}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341952}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "9aa3d544-dbe8-5881-bfea-bb08af9d7f85" :step/math-academy-id 12772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064989}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064991 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064161 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064043 :question/id #uuid "d47e7afd-bed0-5f2b-8635-ddd1a5303837" :question/math-academy-id "e-3719"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392649}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "95f45e70-98bb-552e-8394-58e2d30a7385" :step/math-academy-id 12774 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065167}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065167 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064123 :question/id #uuid "92bba9e0-62db-5c96-8cbc-3cfbe8863a94" :question/math-academy-id "e-6738"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403919}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "4737b469-c8a4-5051-b73e-2b3bd7b686fa" :step/math-academy-id 24870}] :activity/title "The Mean Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064394}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120973} :activity/id #uuid "04524e76-ab3d-5ca9-b72b-ddc9defad7de" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048776 :topic/id #uuid "bdb20c84-9ea4-51a5-8f5e-8982dcb915db" :topic/math-academy-id 1785} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120973 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121072 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have a function $f(x) = x^2+2x,$ and we want to find the derivative of the *inverse* function $(f^{-1})'(x).$ Can we do this without explicitly computing the inverse?\n\nIndeed, we can! The trick is to write the inverse function *implicitly*, differentiate it, and then write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y = x^2+2x,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=y^2+2y\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y=f^{-1}(x).$ Also, remember that swapping $x$ and $y$ gives us the inverse function because the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$ can be obtained by reflecting the graph of $f(x)$ over the line $y=x.$\n\n![](images/56/56c3c733ec9ed00563b5f5c786175898ac6e564658d1a70d4105f749664436ab.png)\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we start by computing $\\dfrac{ \\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}+2y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}+2y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =2y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we take the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}y} = 2y + 2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{2y+2}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x).$ Since $y=f^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2y+2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{2{f}^{-1}(x)+2} \\\\ ({f}^{-1})^{'}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2{f}^{-1}(x)+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "28adec60-b1b0-5bee-949f-2d6ac5eefe88" :tutorial/math-academy-id 443 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0eafffd9-cdc6-547a-8dec-831c2199ceb7" :step/math-academy-id 826 :step/next {: